# The image the musl Linux addons are built in.
#
# The sibling of `Dockerfile.glibc`, and **the reason it is a separate file is
# not the font libraries** -- those are built by the same script, with one
# variable changed. It is that almost everything around them differs: a
# different package manager, a different libc, and an ABI story that does not
# apply at all.
#
# # What does not carry over from the glibc image
#
# **There is no glibc floor here and there cannot be.** musl does not version
# its symbols, so the ceiling apparatus in `check-abi-floor.mjs` has nothing to
# compare and the musl targets carry no `floors` key in `TARGETS` -- absent
# rather than null, because "no ELF floor exists for this libc" is a different
# statement from "unchecked". The acceptance test is the whole of the evidence
# for these targets, which makes it more load-bearing here than on glibc, not
# less.
#
# **The base is Alpine, so `lib` rather than `lib64`.** `install-fontlibs.sh`
# takes that as `LIBDIR` and is otherwise identical between the two families.
#
# **The container does far more work here than on glibc.** rust-skia publishes
# no prebuilt Skia for `*-unknown-linux-musl`, so the download 404s and it
# falls back to compiling Skia from source -- the whole of it, with this
# image's toolchain and `RUSTFLAGS` in play throughout. The glibc container
# gets the prebuilt and never compiles Skia at all: measured in CI, that build
# finished in 7m56s including the docker build, where this one should be
# expected to take 30-45 minutes.
#
# So the two files are less alike than the shared `install-fontlibs.sh`
# suggests. On glibc the image contributes the link and the static font
# libraries; here it additionally contributes every compiler decision Skia is
# built under, which is why the toolchain block below has no glibc counterpart.
#
# `musllinux_1_2_x86_64` is PyPA's, the same reasoning as the glibc image:
# Alpine 3.22 with gcc, g++ and a current cmake already present, maintained by
# people shipping portable native binaries for a living.
# Same reasoning as the glibc image: one file, both architectures.
ARG BASE=quay.io/pypa/musllinux_1_2_x86_64
FROM ${BASE}

# apk rather than dnf, and the package names differ from EL's.
#
# `nasm` is x86-only for the same reason as in the glibc image, and the claim
# was measured there: `libaom-sys` builds on aarch64 with no assembler present,
# because libaom compiles NEON intrinsics rather than assembling them. Same
# `ARG`, same reason -- adding a target should not fork this file.
ARG ASSEMBLER=nasm
RUN apk add --no-cache \
      ${ASSEMBLER} gperf ninja-build meson bash curl tar xz \
      linux-headers musl-dev python3 perl \
      gn clang clang-dev llvm llvm-dev \
      git

# **Skia's own build tools have to come from Alpine, not from skia-bindings.**
#
# `skia-bindings` downloads a prebuilt `gn` and falls back to a bundled one when
# the download fails. Both are glibc x86_64 binaries, so on musl the download
# 404s and the fallback cannot run at all:
#
#   DOWNLOAD AND INSTALL FAILED: curl error code: "22" ... 404
#   rosetta error: failed to open elf at /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2
#
# The build then panics inside `config.rs:350` with an empty message, because
# the assert prints gn's stdout and gn never started. **The failure names
# neither gn nor musl** -- the only readable line is a missing glibc loader,
# reached through a tool nobody asked to download.
#
# Alpine packages both, so they are installed above and skia-bindings is pointed
# at them. `meo-skia-canvas` does the same, for the same reason.
ENV GN_EXE=gn \
    SKIA_GN_COMMAND=/usr/bin/gn \
    SKIA_NINJA_COMMAND=/usr/bin/ninja

# bindgen needs `libclang.so`, and it is a **different package from clang**.
#
# Alpine ships the compiler in `clang` and the library bindgen loads in
# `clang-dev`, under a versioned directory rather than a default search path.
# Without both, Skia compiles all the way through and then:
#
#   panicked at bindgen-0.72.1: Unable to find libclang: "couldn't find any
#   valid shared libraries matching: ['libclang.so', ...]"
#
# `git` is on the list above for the same reason: building Skia from source
# runs `skia/tools/git-sync-deps` to fetch its third-party dependencies, and
# without git that fails as `skia/tools/git-sync-deps failed` with no mention
# of git at all.
#
# **None of this has a glibc counterpart.** The glibc image takes rust-skia's
# prebuilt Skia, which ships prebuilt bindings, so neither bindgen nor
# git-sync-deps ever runs there. Every consequence of building Skia from source
# arrives on this side only, and they arrive one at a time.
ENV LIBCLANG_PATH=/usr/lib/llvm20/lib

# **clang finds no GCC under the Rust triple, and both languages fail
# differently.** Skia's GN build drives `clang++` by name, and `libaom-sys`
# passes `--target=<arch>-unknown-linux-musl` -- the Rust triple. clang locates
# a GCC installation by matching that triple, and Alpine installs its GCC under
# `<arch>-alpine-linux-musl`. Nothing matches, so:
#
#   C:    /usr/bin/ld: cannot find crtbeginS.o
#         /usr/bin/ld: cannot find -lgcc
#   C++:  fatal error: 'cstdint' file not found
#
# Both measured in this image. Without an explicit `--target` the same clang
# compiles and links fine, which is what makes this so easy to miss locally.
#
# Naming the directory answers it, because a directory needs no triple to
# match. It is chosen by **what it contains** rather than by version: the
# complete installation is the one with both `crtbeginS.o` and its C++ headers,
# and "newest" is the wrong test -- `meo-skia-canvas` hit an image carrying a
# second GCC with no headers at all, which clang preferred for being higher.
# This base has exactly one and it is complete, so the loop selects it either
# way and keeps doing so if the base ever gains another.
#
# The config file goes beside the clang binary. `/etc/clang` is not read by
# this one.
#
# The two compiles at the end are the point of the block rather than a
# flourish: they fail the image build here, where the cause is one line above,
# instead of thirty minutes into a Skia compile where the error names a missing
# header.
RUN ARCH="$(uname -m)" && TRIPLE="${ARCH}-alpine-linux-musl" && \
    GCC_DIR="" && \
    for dir in /usr/lib/gcc/"$TRIPLE"/*/; do \
      version="$(basename "$dir")"; \
      if [ -f "${dir}crtbeginS.o" ] && [ -d "/usr/include/c++/${version}/${TRIPLE}" ]; then \
        GCC_DIR="${dir%/}"; \
      fi; \
    done && \
    test -n "$GCC_DIR" && \
    CLANG_DIR="$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$(which clang)")")" && \
    printf -- '--gcc-install-dir=%s\n' "$GCC_DIR" \
      | tee "$CLANG_DIR/clang++.cfg" > "$CLANG_DIR/clang.cfg" && \
    printf 'int main(){ return 0; }\n' > /tmp/t.c && \
    printf '#include <cstdint>\n#include <vector>\nint main(){ std::vector<int> v; return (int)v.size(); }\n' > /tmp/t.cc && \
    clang --target="${ARCH}-unknown-linux-musl" /tmp/t.c -o /tmp/t && \
    clang++ --target="${ARCH}-unknown-linux-musl" /tmp/t.cc -o /tmp/txx && \
    rm -f /tmp/t.c /tmp/t.cc /tmp/t /tmp/txx && \
    echo "clang compiles and links C and C++ under the rust triple: ok"

# Identical to the glibc image apart from where Alpine puts libraries.
#
# The static font libraries matter more here rather than less: an Alpine image
# is chosen for being small, so it is even less likely to carry fontconfig than
# the slim Debian images that already did not.
COPY install-fontlibs.sh /opt/install-fontlibs.sh
RUN LIBDIR=lib bash /opt/install-fontlibs.sh && rm -f /opt/install-fontlibs.sh

ENV PKG_CONFIG_ALL_STATIC=1

# The same flag and the same trap as the glibc image: rustc links through `cc`,
# which drops `-static-libstdc++` without a word, so the linker is pointed at
# `g++` to make it a C++ link.
#
# What it buys differs. On glibc it pins the residue to an old GLIBCXX baseline
# so the binary loads on RHEL 8. Here there is no such baseline to reach for --
# Alpine has one libstdc++ and no version skew across releases -- so the value
# is simply that less of it has to be present at runtime.
# `-crt-static` is the musl-specific half, and without it there is no addon at
# all. Rust's musl targets default to `crt-static`, which produces a
# statically-linked *executable* and **cannot produce a `cdylib`**:
#
#   error: cannot produce cdylib for `meo-canvas-node` as the target
#          `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl` does not support these crate types
#
# A Node addon is a shared object by definition, so the default is simply wrong
# for this artefact. Turning it off makes musl behave like every other target
# here: a dynamically linked `.so` against the system libc, which on Alpine is
# musl and is present by construction.
#
# This is the clearest example of why musl is a second instance of this work
# rather than a variant of the glibc image. Nothing in the glibc build has an
# equivalent, and no amount of care over there would have surfaced it.
ENV RUSTFLAGS="-C linker=g++ -C link-arg=-static-libstdc++ -C target-feature=-crt-static"

ARG RUST=1.98.0
RUN curl -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh -s -- -y --profile minimal --default-toolchain "$RUST"
ENV PATH="/root/.cargo/bin:${PATH}"

WORKDIR /src
